input.content:
{
  "auflegen": {
    "findings": [
      {
        "type": "bad_gloss",
        "severity": "medium",
        "detail": "The shipped 'lay on' is kaikki's first-listed literal sense, but the reading a learner actually meets is telephonic: 'Ich lege jetzt auf' (I'm hanging up now) is the everyday use, and candidate_glosses lists 'to hang up' at index 1. The DJ sense (Platten auflegen, index 4) is also more current than bare 'lay on'.",
        "fix_gloss": "hang up, lay on"
      }
    ]
  },
  "auflehnen": {
    "findings": [
      {
        "type": "bad_gloss",
        "severity": "medium",
        "detail": "In actual use auflehnen is essentially always reflexive and means to rebel or revolt: 'sich gegen die Obrigkeit auflehnen'. The physical 'lean one's arms on' reading behind the shipped 'lean on' is marginal and, as a bare gloss, collides with anlehnen and sich stützen auf. candidate_glosses has the right sense at index 1.",
        "fix_gloss": "rebel, revolt"
      }
    ]
  },
  "aufschieben": {
    "findings": [
      {
        "type": "bad_gloss",
        "severity": "medium",
        "detail": "The dominant sense is temporal — to postpone or defer, as in 'die Entscheidung aufschieben' (hence Aufschieberitis for procrastination). The shipped 'push open' is the literal spatial reading, restricted to things that slide (a bolt, a sliding door), and is much rarer. candidate_glosses lists 'to postpone' at index 1.",
        "fix_gloss": "postpone, put off"
      }
    ]
  },
  "aufschlagen": {
    "findings": [
      {
        "type": "bad_gloss",
        "severity": "medium",
        "detail": "'pitch, serve' ships kaikki's first sense, a narrow sports reading, and in English the pair is doubly opaque because 'pitch' reads as baseball rather than as the tennis serve intended. The senses a learner meets are 'ein Buch aufschlagen' (open a book — candidate index 3) and 'auf dem Boden aufschlagen' (hit, impact — index 5).",
        "fix_gloss": "open (a book), serve"
      }
    ]
  },
  "aufsetzen": {
    "findings": [
      {
        "type": "bad_gloss",
        "severity": "low",
        "detail": "The shortening dropped the part that carried the meaning: candidate index 0 is 'to put on (something to be worn on the head)'. Bare 'put on' invites the learner to use aufsetzen for clothing generally, which takes anziehen — one aufsetzt a Hut, Mütze, or Brille, not Schuhe.",
        "fix_gloss": "put on (headwear)"
      }
    ]
  }
}

